\subsection{Outcomes}

I am primarily interested in the effect of board turnover on two classes of 
outcomes. First, and most directly, boards are charged with hiring and monitoring 
the performance of a district administrator who manages the academic and 
financial affairs of the district. The choice of superintendent is critical to 
the board as the superintendent is the board's primary conduit of information 
about the health of the district and the educational strategies in place for 
students. Superintendent turnover is a primary prediction of dissatisfaction 
theory, and rightfully so. If voters are unhappy with the state of affairs in 
their school district, in all likelihood they are unhappy with the performance 
of their district administrator. 

More indirectly, school boards are responsible for setting policy and directing 
the superintendent to provide quality education. Thus, incumbent defeat may 
serve as a method by which the community can express its demand for higher 
quality education as measured by accountability measures such as student 
test scores and graduation rates. 


\section{Methods}

Measuring the impact of school board turnout on superintendent turnover and 
student outcomes is difficult because of the large number of competing 
forces shaping these outcomes. Student performance is largely predicted by 
family income and parental education levels. Superintendent turnover may be 
related to salary and the prestige associated with leading certain school 
districts. In order to better understand the impact of school board turnover 
net of these factors, I employ a regression strategy to control for as many 
of these observable and unobservable factors as possible. 


I employ two distinct modeling strategies. The first is the most restrictive. 
As in previous chapters, I employ the bounding strategy suggested by \citet{mhe} 
to leverage the repeated observations in my data to compare, where possible, 
school districts within themselves. The disadvantage of this strategy is that 
for the outcomes in question, relatively little variance remains to be explained 
after accounting for all between school district effects, because the within 
school district variation is so small. 

The second strategy is more susceptible to unobserved alternative explanatory 
variables, but attempts to explain the variation in outcomes between school 
districts by comparing school districts with similar demographic 
charcteristics, but which differ on their incumbent defeat. 


\section{Results}


\subsection{Student Outcomes}

<<testScoresR, results='asis'>>=
modeldata <- dist_turn[, c("distid", "year", "incsDefeated", "servingMembers",
                           "READ_PROFADV_PER", "NEW_SUP", "SUP_EXPERIENCE",
                           "econ_disadv_per", "VAP_adj", "per65o", 
                           "PerBachelorOrAbove", "locale2")]
modeldata$incsDefeatedFac <- ifelse(modeldata$incsDefeated > 0, 1, 0)
modeldata$incsDefeatedFac <- factor(modeldata$incsDefeatedFac)
preTreat <- modeldata[modeldata$year <= 2010,]
preTreat$year <- preTreat$year + 2
names(preTreat) <- paste(names(preTreat), "lag", sep = "_")
modeldata <- modeldata[, c("READ_PROFADV_PER", "distid", "year")]
modeldata <- merge(modeldata, preTreat, by.y = c("distid_lag", "year_lag"), 
                 by.x = c("distid", "year"))
modeldata <- modeldata[modeldata$year > 2005, ]
modeldata$SUP_EXPERIENCE_lag <- ifelse(is.na(modeldata$SUP_EXPERIENCE_lag), 0, 
                                       modeldata$SUP_EXPERIENCE_lag)

simpModRead <- lm(READ_PROFADV_PER ~ incsDefeatedFac_lag + locale2_lag  +
                    servingMembers_lag, 
                  data = modeldata)
simpModRead$se <- sqrt(diag(get_CL_vcov(simpModRead, modeldata$distid)))
lagModRead <- lm(READ_PROFADV_PER ~ incsDefeatedFac_lag + econ_disadv_per_lag + 
                   READ_PROFADV_PER_lag + servingMembers_lag + 
                   log(VAP_adj_lag) + locale2_lag + PerBachelorOrAbove_lag + 
                   per65o_lag, data = modeldata)
lagModRead$se <- sqrt(diag(get_CL_vcov(lagModRead, 
                   modeldata$distid[!is.na(modeldata$READ_PROFADV_PER_lag)])))
lagModReadFE <- lm(READ_PROFADV_PER ~ incsDefeatedFac_lag + econ_disadv_per_lag + 
                   log(VAP_adj_lag) + locale2_lag + PerBachelorOrAbove_lag + 
                      servingMembers_lag + per65o_lag + distid, 
                   data = modeldata)
lagModReadFE$se <- sqrt(diag(get_CL_vcov(lagModReadFE, modeldata$distid)))

apsrtable(simpModRead, lagModRead, lagModReadFE, se = "robust", 
          stars = "default", digits = 3, model.names = c("Lag"), 
          caption = "Incumbent Defeat and Student Scores", 
          label = "tab:readMod", notes = list(stars.note), 
          Sweave = TRUE, 
           omitcoef=list("distid", 
                         expression(grep("distid", coefnames, value=TRUE))))
@



